@meta
id: 02-07
title: Drill — botch it, undo it
chapter: 02
type: drill
skills: undo, write
par: 26
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: type, Ctrl+Z, type again
open: queue.md

@brief
Eight times, the same three seconds: append ` TODO` to the line, undo it,
append ` (done)` instead, write and quit.

Nothing to work out. What you are building is the reflex of reaching for
`u` the moment an edit looks wrong, instead of staring at it.

@keys
| `$a` | append at the end of the line |
| `u` | undo the last change |
| `ZZ` | write and quit |

@hints
`Esc` before `u`, every time. In insert mode `u` is just a letter.
--
After the undo the cursor is back on the line that changed, so `$a` works
straight away without moving first.

@solution
    $ a  TODO <Esc> u $ a  (done) <Esc> ZZ

Twenty-two keystrokes, and by rep four you should not be reading them.

Watch what `u` does to the cursor: it puts you on the line it changed.
That is deliberate, and it is why the second `$a` needs no navigation.
Vim's undo is not just a rewind of the text, it is a rewind of where you
were, which is what makes it usable in the middle of a thought.

@start queue.md
- Reconcile the payment batch

@expected queue.md
- Reconcile the payment batch (done)
